# 🚨 Error Handling

## Core Principles
- Fail gracefully: no raw errors/stack traces to users
- Provide context: clear explanations + next steps
- Log with context for debugging
- Offer recovery options
- Always handle or propagate errors

## Error Boundaries (React)
- Wrap route segments + critical features
- Provide fallback UI with retry option
- Log to monitoring service
- Component-level boundaries (don't just wrap root)

## Async Handling
- Always wrap in try-catch
- Check response.ok before parsing
- Return/throw user-friendly messages
- Server Actions: return `{success, data/error}`

## API Errors
- Consistent format: `{success, data?, error?, code?}`
- Proper HTTP status codes:
  - 200 (Success), 201 (Created), 400 (Bad Request)
  - 401 (Unauthorized), 403 (Forbidden), 404 (Not Found)
  - 422 (Validation Error), 500 (Server Error)
- Validation errors: include field-level details

## User Messages
- Clear, actionable language
- Network: "Unable to connect. Check your internet."
- Validation: "Email is required"
- Permission: "You don't have permission."
- Not found: "We couldn't find that page."
- Server: "Something went wrong on our end."

## Forms
- Field-level errors next to inputs
- Use `aria-invalid` and `aria-describedby`
- Form-level error summary in `role="alert"`

## States
- Always handle: loading, error, empty, success
- Fallback content for failures
- Degrade gracefully for optional features

## Logging
- Include context: userId, timestamp, relevant data
- Never log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, cards)
- Send to monitoring service (Sentry, etc.)

## Retry Logic
- Exponential backoff for network errors
- Don't retry 4xx client errors
- Retry 5xx server errors

## Checklist
- [ ] Error boundaries in place
- [ ] Try-catch on async operations
- [ ] User-friendly messages
- [ ] All states handled (loading/error/empty)
- [ ] Field-level form errors
- [ ] Context-rich logging
- [ ] No sensitive data in logs
